.TH DAYTONA-GREETER 1 "November 2026" "Daytona 0.2.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
daytona-greeter \- Daytona's log on screen for greetd
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B daytona-greeter
[\fIlabwc options\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B daytona-greeter
is a greeter for \fBgreetd\fR(1): a Windows 98-style log on dialog with
User name, Password, a Session... button for the desktop to start and a
Shut Down... button. greetd runs it as its greeter user. It starts labwc
with the log on screen's own configuration
(\fI/usr/share/daytona-greeter/labwc\fR) on the system's keyboard layout
(systemd-localed's, else \fI/etc/default/keyboard\fR), and labwc runs the
dialog, \fI/usr/libexec/daytona/daytona-logon\fR, which talks to greetd over
\fBGREETD_SOCK\fR.
.PP
To use it, set it as greetd's greeter in \fI/etc/greetd/config.toml\fR:
.PP
.nf
.RS
[default_session]
command = "daytona-greeter"
user = "_greetd"
.RE
.fi
.PP
Sessions are read from the \fIwayland-sessions\fR directories of
\fBXDG_DATA_DIRS\fR. The last user name and session are kept in
\fI/var/lib/daytona-greeter/state\fR (\fBDAYTONA_GREETER_STATE\fR to
override); passwords are never stored.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/share/doc/daytona-greeter/examples/config.toml
An example greetd configuration.
.TP
.I /usr/share/doc/daytona-greeter/greeter.md
How to install it in place of another display manager.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR greetd (1),
.BR daytona-session (1),
.BR labwc (1)
